# Grafana Cloud instance the dashboards are pushed to, and a service account
# token with dashboard write scope. Both are REQUIRED by the two push scripts —
# neither has a default any more.
GRAFANA_URL=https://your-org.grafana.net
GRAFANA_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_TOKEN=glsa_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# uid of the Grafana PostgreSQL datasource the dashboards read (role grafana_ro,
# schema dash — every panel queries a dash.feed_* view). This repository is
# PUBLIC, so the checked-in dashboards never carry that uid: they carry the
# placeholder ${DS_LINKEDIN_PG}. `push-dashboard.mjs --file` swaps the uid in
# just before the POST and REFUSES to push (exit 2, nothing sent) when this is
# unset or is not a plain uid; `--dump` swaps it back out and REFUSES (exit 2,
# nothing written) when the live dashboard reads Postgres and this is unset —
# without the uid in hand only the { type, uid } form could be recognised, and a
# type-less or legacy reference would land in the file as it is. Both directions
# need it. update-post-variable.mjs does not.
# OPERATOR-LOCAL ONLY: no workflow pushes dashboards, so this is NOT an Actions
# secret or variable — it lives in this gitignored .env and nowhere else.
# Where to find it: Grafana -> Connections -> Data sources -> the PostgreSQL one;
# the uid is the last segment of that page's URL.
# Left commented on purpose: an unset variable makes the push refuse, a made-up
# one would push seven dashboards pointing at a datasource that does not exist.
# GRAFANA_PG_DATASOURCE_UID=your-datasource-uid

# Folder uid for dashboards this repo creates. Every board of ours lives in one
# folder; without this a NEW author's dashboard is created in General instead
# (a warning says so, it never fails the onboarding). An EXISTING dashboard is
# left in its own folder unless you pass --move. The uid is the last path
# segment of the folder's URL: /dashboards/f/<uid>/
# GRAFANA_FOLDER_UID=your-folder-uid

# Slack channel id. ONE NAME, TWO READERS — check which one you are setting:
#   - run-weekly.sh maps it to PL_SLACK_CHANNEL_ID (run-weekly.sh:196) for the
#     run-started / run-finished bookends. Local only; CI never posts these.
#   - notify-session-check.mjs REQUIRES it, and for that one it must be the
#     C… id of #linkedin-session-bot specifically, because that is the channel
#     whose membership is scoped to the four authors plus the operator.
# They are different channels doing different jobs, and nothing warns you if
# you cross them: the session bot would simply @-tag four people in whatever
# channel this points at, together with a link that controls their LinkedIn.
# Locally that is this one variable, so set it to whichever you are testing.
# In CI they cannot collide — the bookends do not run there — and both the
# daily session check and the weekly result message (notify-weekly.mjs, same
# bot, same channel) read the Actions VARIABLE of the same name.
# Leave unset to run run-weekly.sh without any Slack posting.
SLACK_CHANNEL_ID=

# --- Daily session monitor (.github/workflows/linkedin-session-check.yml)
# Once a day the workflow checks whether each author is still logged into
# LinkedIn and posts, EVERY run, a status roster: one line per account with its
# login state, no @-mentions at all. For anyone who is logged out it then posts
# a second message that @-tags them with a fresh Live View re-login link.
# The roster is context; the tagged message is the thing to act on, and only
# people who have to act are ever tagged. Every string the channel sees is in
# English. Read by .github/scripts/notify-session-check.mjs; in CI these are an
# Actions secret (the token) and Actions variables (the rest), of the same names.

# REQUIRED by the notifier. A bot token, xoxb-…, with EXACTLY the scopes
# chat:write and chat:write.public — no user scopes, no im:*. The bot cannot
# and must not be able to DM: everything it says belongs in the channel where
# the operator can see it too. Without it the notifier exits 2.
# Навмисно без зразка значення: будь-який рядок у формі xoxb-<цифри>-<цифри>-<...>
# ловить GitHub push protection як справжній токен і блокує пуш усього репо.
# SLACK_BOT_TOKEN=

# Maps lifleet slug -> Slack member id, so the message can @-tag the right
# person: {"peter":"U01ABC","andy":"U02DEF","maria":"U03GHI"}
# An "_operator" key carries the operator's id, and it is the one entry that is
# not an author: it @-tags the person who owns the repo checkout and the
# Browserbase key on the message about work only they can do — minting a link
# by hand when the run could not. Without it that message still posts, just
# without a ping. Authors never get repo commands: the account owner has no
# checkout, no key and no rights, so an instruction addressed to them reads as
# a broken bot and gets done by nobody.
#
# NEVER COMMIT THE REAL MAP, and never turn it into a checked-in JSON file:
# this repository is PUBLIC, so a committed map is a permanent published list
# of who works here and which Slack account is theirs. It lives in this
# gitignored .env locally and in an Actions secret or variable in CI.
#
# Optional, and it degrades PER ENTRY. Unset, a slug missing from it, or a
# value that is not a member id (a "@handle" instead of a U…) all fall back to
# posting that ONE person's plain name — which posts but does NOT notify them.
# Everybody else keeps their ping: one typo must never be able to cancel the
# alert for the person who really is logged out. So does an id for someone who
# is not a member of the channel: Slack does not notify a non-member, the run
# still goes green, and nobody is paged.
# The one fatal shape is a value that is not a JSON object at all — then there
# is no map to degrade from, and both the workflow preflight and the notifier
# stop rather than post an alert that pings nobody.
# SLACK_PEOPLE_JSON={"peter":"U02DEF"}

# SLACK_REPORT_ALL is GONE. It used to turn an all-live day from silence into a
# one-line all-clear; the status roster now posts on every run, so there is
# nothing left for it to switch on. It was deliberately not repurposed into a
# "suppress the roster" flag: the same name with the opposite meaning is a trap
# for anyone who remembers the old one, and a switch that silences the daily
# status is just the old silence under a new name. Setting it does nothing.

# Optional. "1" prints the Slack payloads to stdout and posts nothing —
# for trying message wording out without pinging four people.
# CAUTION: the printed payload contains the Live View URL, which is live
# control of that person's logged-in LinkedIn browser. Keep dry runs on your
# own machine. The workflow's dry_run input is safe because it also passes
# --no-invite, so no link is ever minted to be printed; a dry run you drive by
# hand from a report that HAS links has no such protection.
# SLACK_DRY_RUN=

# Chrome profile the scraper drives. Defaults to the macOS ms-playwright cache
# (~/Library/Caches/ms-playwright/mcp-chrome-linkedin-stats); set this on Linux or
# when pointing the run at a sandbox profile.
# LI_CHROME_PROFILE_DIR=

# --- Browserbase backend (LI_BACKEND=browserbase, the default in run-weekly.sh)
# The scraper drives a REMOTE browser, so these two are REQUIRED for any weekly
# run; run-weekly.sh now refuses to start without them. Locally they live in
# scripts/lifleet/.env (source it, or copy them here); in CI they are Actions
# secrets of the SAME two names. See .claude/skills/linkedin-stats/WEEKLY-CADENCE.md.
# BROWSERBASE_API_KEY=
# BROWSERBASE_PROJECT_ID=

# Path to the lifleet registry that maps author slug -> Browserbase context_id.
# The default is scripts/lifleet/authors.json, which is GITIGNORED — a fresh
# checkout has none, so CI materialises it from the LIFLEET_AUTHORS_JSON secret
# outside the working tree and points this at that file. LIFLEET_REGISTRY is
# accepted as an alias (that is the name scripts/lifleet/.env uses).
# LIFLEET_AUTHORS=

# Optional: `off` skips the proxy request that 402s on the free Browserbase plan.
# LIFLEET_PROXIES=on

# --- Database (Supabase Postgres). The dashboards read the DB, so these are the
# operator's keys to the same data Grafana shows. FIVE roles, five DSNs, and the
# difference matters — each script takes the weakest one that can do its job:
#   LI_DATABASE_URL            the OWNER (postgres.<project-ref>). Only
#                              apply-schema.mjs and a --reset import need it.
#   LI_SYNC_DATABASE_URL       li_sync: SELECT + INSERT into two tables +
#                              EXECUTE on the merge functions. What CI uses for
#                              the weekly sync, and what verify.mjs runs as.
#   LI_GRAFANA_RO_DATABASE_URL grafana_ro: SELECT on the dash views only. Run
#                              verify-panels.mjs as this one — it is the role the
#                              dashboards themselves use.
#   LI_BACKUP_DATABASE_URL     li_backup: read-only, everything pg_dump reads.
#   LI_WRITER_DATABASE_URL     li_writer: the scraper's role (kept for the
#                              dual-write stage; nothing local needs it daily).
# Connection: the SESSION pooler host (aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:5432,
# user <role>.<project-ref>) — the direct host is IPv6-only, and the transaction
# pooler breaks prepared statements. TLS is verified in full against
# db/certs/supabase-root-2021.crt; never disable it.
# In CI the sync and backup DSNs are Actions secrets of the same names; the
# owner DSN is NOT in CI and must not be — nothing automated may apply a schema.
# LI_DATABASE_URL=
# LI_SYNC_DATABASE_URL=
# LI_GRAFANA_RO_DATABASE_URL=
# LI_BACKUP_DATABASE_URL=
# LI_WRITER_DATABASE_URL=

# The plain password of grafana_ro, for pasting into the Grafana datasource form
# by hand. Not read by any script — it is here so the operator has one place to
# look. Everything automated uses the DSNs above.
# LI_GRAFANA_RO_PASSWORD=
